hi I'm Alec Baldwin and I wanted to  begin the tributes tonight by saying  that as an actor I dreamed years ago of  being both talented enough and lucky  enough to have a specific type of career  to move almost at will between stage and  screen and to leave a powerful memorable  impression with the force and dexterity  of one's work to play barbarians and  kings champions and chauffeurs the  apotheosis of the dark side and a cold  warrior in American intelligence two  equally incalculable effect I wanted  James Earl Jones's career that is what I  wanted that is what every actor wants  some actors become stars who burn  brightly for a distinct period of time  that enter a phase where they try hard  to get it back James Earl Jones never  had to get it back because he never lost  it from his first film role in Kubrick's  dr. Strangelove in 1964 through his Tony  award-winning performance in the great  white hope on Broadway and then through  decades of staggering roles from a  Shakespeare to Star Wars James Earl  Jones is one of the greatest actors in  American history and an example to all  and an example to all by doing not by  saying but by doing when I worked with  James in the movie Hunt for Red October  in 1989 I waited for that moment when I  could have him alone to pick his brains  to ask the great man for some advice and  we were standing outside standing  outside a stage at Paramount and the  moment came and we were smoking a  cigarette together out on the alleyway  and I finally said to him what advice  would you give to me as an actor and he  stared at me with that face of his and  took the longest pause you could ever  measure and he said what advice would I  give you as an actor and my heart was  racing I said yes yes what advice would  you give me as an actor and he said my  advice to you would be to quit smoking  and all the rest of it I learned just  from watching James Earl Jones  congratulations to James Earl Jones  you  you
 

